Questionnaire and Behavioral Data Comparison
In the last stage of the analysis, we aimed to determine the extent to which selfreported measures, such as anxiety, trust, and preferred methods of control, correlate with participants’ objective behavior during the experiment. To investigate this, we conducted Spearman’s rank-order correlation to assess the relationship between reaction time and dwell time with questionnaire scales from factor analysis. The correlation between reaction time and Anxiety_Trust factor was found to be non-significant. Similarly, the correlations between reaction time and Methods_of_Control_eye, Methods_of_Control_hand, and Methods_of_Control_feet were also non-significant, indicating no meaningful relationships between reaction time and these variables. A similar series of Spearman’s rank analyses was conducted for dwell time, which also showed no significant correlation with the questionnaire measures (see Figure 10 for correlation scores and significance levels). Overall, these findings reveal that, in the context of semi-autonomous studies, self-reported measures of acceptance do not reliably correlate with users’ actual behavior while driving.
